Abstract

This thesis proposes a theoretical cyber-physical architecture for magnetic road networks with ground-level permanent-magnet electrodynamic suspension (PMEDS), coupled to a deterministic AI-Grid Safety Kernel (AGSK) that validates every command proposed by multi-agent AI against physically derived constraints. The work is purely theoretical: no prototype has been built, no measurement taken, and no simulation run. Every number is a closed-form estimate, a design target or a parameter assumption, and carries a status from the taxonomy [H] hypothesis, [C] coherent, [S] simulated, [V] verified. Version 2.0 claims no [S] result.

The thesis has four parts. Part I (Chapters 2–4) states viability, invariance and barrier-certificate theory; the invariance theorem is restated as a sufficient condition plus a necessary condition at a point, with a counterexample showing why the earlier equality fails. Part II (Chapter 5) defines the Physical Reference Model as nine gates, P1.0–P1.8, each with a validity domain, a parameter band and a status. Part III (Chapters 6–12) develops one frozen electrical design case: corridor load, substation allocation under N−1, a 3000 V DC bus, a 25 mm² preliminary cable, protection and braking timing, storage, and the resilience problem. Part IV (Chapters 13–15) specifies the three-layer control hierarchy, the AGSK verification contract and the threat model. Chapter 16 states a falsification programme with explicit pass/fail semantics; Chapter 17 concludes.

Under the reference parameters the per-vehicle load lies in 2.19–6.36 kW (central 3.26 kW), the 1 km corridor load in 44–127 kW, and the speed bound from a 50 m headway is 12.9 m/s. These are conditional model outputs, not empirical bounds.

The central rule: no artificial intelligence shall be tasked with compensating for physics that has not yet been understood, and no safety constraint shall be declared operative unless it is derivable from a physical result with explicitly bounded domain and uncertainty.

This is a corrective revision of the earlier "Final Theoretical Revision". Section 1.7 and Appendix B list every correction.

1. Introduction

1.1 Problem statement

Existing maglev systems offer engineering principles for power supply, but moving them to a road network for wheel-less vehicles needs a dedicated architecture. Two reference points from the earlier text are kept as context, both [H] until sourced: a high-speed maglev line served by large substations with AC to DC conversion every few kilometres, and an Inductrack-type urban train of 20,000 kg at 50 km/h with a lift-to-drag ratio of 30, giving a drag force near 6,500 N and a mechanical drag power above 90 kW. Both figures need citations before submission. The earlier text also paired a 50 MVA substation rating with a 60 MW peak load, which is inconsistent as written and is not reused.

An urban road corridor differs in scale: lower speed, lighter vehicles, short corridors, denser traffic and road-safety duties. At low speed, eddy-current drag dominates and the lift-to-drag ratio is far below its high-speed asymptote.

In parallel, multi-agent AI for urban infrastructure creates a new risk: agents that optimise energy, transport and buildings may propose commands that are locally coherent but globally unsafe. The thesis therefore treats the electrical architecture and the control architecture as one cyber-physical problem.

