Part I — Mathematical Foundations

2. Preliminaries: Dynamical Systems and Control

2.1 Control system

Definition 2.1 (control system). A control system is a triple Σ = (f, U, D) with dynamics f: R^n × U × D → R^n, a compact control set U ⊂ R^m and a compact disturbance set D ⊂ R^p. Trajectories solve:

\dot{x}(t) = f(x(t), u(t), d(t)), \qquad x(0) = x_0

Assumption 2.1. f is continuous, f is Lipschitz in (x, u) uniformly in d, and f has linear growth in x. Then for every measurable u(·) and d(·) with values in U and D, the solution exists for all t ≥ 0 and is unique. The earlier text required Lipschitz continuity in x only; Lipschitz continuity in u is added because Section 3.4 composes f with a feedback μ.

2.2 Constraint set and tangent cone

Definition 2.2 (constraint set). K = { x : g_i(x) ≤ 0, i = 1…N_c } is closed, with each g_i continuously differentiable.

Definition 2.3 (tangent cone). The (Bouligand) tangent cone of K at x ∈ K is:

T_K(x) = \mathrm{cl}\Big( \bigcup_{h>0} \frac{K - x}{h} \Big)

If the gradients of the active constraints at x are linearly independent, then T_K(x) = { v : ∇g_i(x)·v ≤ 0 for every active i }. At an interior point T_K(x) = R^n.

3. Viability, Invariance and Discriminating Kernels

3.1 Viability kernel

Definition 3.1. Viab(K) is the set of x_0 ∈ K for which some control u(·) with values in U keeps the disturbance-free trajectory in K for all t ≥ 0. This is an open-loop notion and ignores disturbances.

3.2 Robust invariance kernel

Definition 3.2 (robust controlled invariance). A set C ⊆ K is robustly controlled invariant if there is a feedback μ: N(C) → U, locally Lipschitz on an open neighbourhood N(C) of C, such that every solution of ẋ = f(x, μ(x), d(t)) with x(0) ∈ C and any measurable d(·) in D exists for all t ≥ 0 and stays in C.

The robust invariance kernel Inv(K) is the union of all robustly controlled invariant subsets of K. Each initial state may use its own feedback, so Inv(K) need not itself admit one feedback. This is why the AGSK works with an explicit invariant subset C and its feedback (Chapter 14).

3.3 Discriminating kernel

Definition 3.3. Disc(K) is the set of x_0 ∈ K for which there is a non-anticipative strategy β, mapping disturbance signals to control signals so that u(t) depends on d(s) for s ≤ t only, keeping every trajectory in K. The earlier quantifier order "for all d, there exists u" let the control see the whole future disturbance; that is not implementable and is replaced. In general:

\mathrm{Inv}(K) \subseteq \mathrm{Disc}(K) \subseteq K

Inv(K) is the relevant kernel for the safety layer because the feedback is fixed before the disturbance is known and the AGSK sees measurements with delay. Disc(K) is a less conservative reference.

3.4 Invariance: sufficient condition, necessary condition at a point

Define the pointwise robust tangency set:

\mathcal{T}(K) = \{ x \in K : \exists u \in U,\ \forall d \in D,\ f(x,u,d) \in T_K(x) \}

Theorem 3.1 (corrected). Under Assumption 2.1:

(a) Sufficiency. Let C ⊆ K be closed and let μ be locally Lipschitz on a neighbourhood of C with f(x, μ(x), d) ∈ T_C(x) for all x ∈ C and all d ∈ D. Then C is robustly controlled invariant, so C ⊆ Inv(K).

(b) Necessity at a point. If x_0 ∈ Inv(K) through a feedback μ that is continuous at x_0, then x_0 ∈ 𝒯(K).

(c) Hence Inv(K) ⊆ 𝒯(K). Equality is not claimed and fails in general (Counterexample 3.1). Status [V] under the stated hypotheses.

Proof of (a). Set F(x) = { f(x, μ(x), d) : d ∈ D }. For x, y near C, sup over d of |f(x, μ(x), d) − f(y, μ(y), d)| ≤ L(1 + L_μ)|x − y|, so F is Lipschitz in the Hausdorff metric with compact values. For such a map, a closed set is strongly invariant (every solution of the inclusion stays in it) when ⟨ζ, v⟩ ≤ 0 for all v ∈ F(x) and every proximal normal ζ to C at x (Bony–Brezis; Clarke–Ledyaev–Stern–Wolenski, Ch. 4; Aubin–Cellina; Blanchini–Miani). Every v ∈ T_C(x) satisfies this inequality, because the polar of the proximal normal cone contains the Bouligand cone. The hypothesis F(x) ⊆ T_C(x) is therefore sufficient. ∎

Proof of (b). Fix d ∈ D and take the constant disturbance d(t) ≡ d. Since μ is continuous at x_0, the solution φ is differentiable from the right at 0 with derivative f(x_0, μ(x_0), d). Because φ(h) ∈ K for h ≥ 0, the quotients (φ(h) − x_0)/h lie in (K − x_0)/h, so the limit lies in T_K(x_0). Hence f(x_0, μ(x_0), d) ∈ T_K(x_0) for every d, which gives x_0 ∈ 𝒯(K) with u = μ(x_0). ∎

Counterexample 3.1 (why equality fails). Take the double integrator ẋ_1 = x_2, ẋ_2 = u, |u| ≤ 1, no disturbance, and K = { x_1 ≤ 0 }. Every interior point has T_K = R², so (−0.1, 5) ∈ 𝒯(K). But x_2(t) ≥ 5 − t, hence x_1(t) ≥ −0.1 + 5t − t²/2, which is 0.395 at t = 0.1. The state leaves K for every control, so (−0.1, 5) ∉ Inv(K) and not in Viab(K) either.

The earlier proof failed in two places. The first inclusion applied Nagumo's theorem to all of K while tangency was assumed only on a subset. The second inclusion started from one invariant state and then argued for every x ∈ K.

3.5 Barrier certificates

Theorem 3.2 (corrected). Let B be continuously differentiable with K = { B ≤ 0 } and ∇B(x) ≠ 0 whenever B(x) = 0. Suppose a feedback μ, locally Lipschitz on a neighbourhood of K, satisfies:

\nabla B(x)\cdot f(x,\mu(x),d) \le 0 \quad \text{for all } x \text{ with } B(x)=0,\ \text{all } d \in D

Then K is robustly controlled invariant, so Inv(K) = K. Status [V] under these hypotheses.

Proof. Where B(x) < 0, x is interior and T_K(x) = R^n. Where B(x) = 0 with ∇B(x) ≠ 0, T_K(x) is the half-space { v : ∇B(x)·v ≤ 0 }, which contains f(x, μ(x), d) by hypothesis. Tangency therefore holds at every point of K, and Theorem 3.1(a) with C = K gives the claim. ∎

Remark 3.1 (what the theorem does not give). Pointwise existence of some u at each boundary point does not by itself yield a locally Lipschitz μ. A strict barrier inequality plus a constraint qualification and convex U can give a Lipschitz min-norm feedback (Ames et al. 2017; Jankovic 2018; hypotheses to be checked case by case). Without it, Theorem 3.2 is not applied.

Proposition 3.1 (under-approximation). Let K_δ = { B ≤ −δ } for some δ > 0, assume ∇B ≠ 0 on { B = −δ }, and assume a locally Lipschitz μ with ∇B·f(x, μ(x), d) ≤ 0 on { B = −δ } for all d. Then K_δ ⊆ Inv(K). Proof. Apply Theorem 3.2 to B + δ, noting K_δ ⊆ K. ∎

Definition 3.4 (reachable set). Reach(X_0, T) is the set of all φ(t; x_0, u, d) with x_0 ∈ X_0, t ∈ [0, T] and admissible u, d, under a stated feedback or an open-loop control set.

4. Uncertainty Propagation and Constraint Margins

4.1 Interval arithmetic

An interval [x] = [x_lo, x_hi] is a closed bounded subset of R. An interval extension [g] of g satisfies g(x) ∈ g for every x in [x].

Theorem 4.1 (fundamental theorem of interval arithmetic, Moore). If g is a finite composition of arithmetic operations and elementary functions, each defined on the intervals involved (no division by an interval containing 0), its natural interval extension is inclusion-isotone and encloses the range: g([x]) ⊆ g. Status [V], classical result (Moore, Kearfott and Cloud 2009), not a contribution of this thesis.

4.2 Constraint margins

Definition 4.3 (margin). For c(x) ≤ 0 with |c − c_nom| ≤ ε, the robust constraint is c_nom(x) ≤ −ε.

Theorem 4.2 (margin sufficiency). If c_nom(x) ≤ −ε over the uncertainty set, then c(x_actual) ≤ 0. Proof. c(x_actual) ≤ c_nom + ε ≤ −ε + ε = 0. ∎ Status [V].

Proposition 4.3 (timing margin). Let the AGSK check constraint g_i every T_s, let the check-to-effect latency be τ, and let |d g_i(x(t))/dt| ≤ Λ_i on admissible trajectories. If the measurement error is at most ε_i^meas, then the margin must satisfy:

\varepsilon_i \ge \varepsilon_i^{\mathrm{meas}} + \Lambda_i\,(T_s + \tau)

Proof. Between a check and the earliest effect of the next decision, g_i can move by at most Λ_i (T_s + τ); the measured value is off by at most ε_i^meas. ∎ Status [V] conditional on the bound Λ_i, which is [H].

4.3 Application to AGSK constraints

For each physical constraint g_i(x) ≤ 0, the AGSK constraint is g_i(x_meas) + ε_i ≤ 0, with ε_i from Proposition 4.3 and the gate that supplies the parameter uncertainty. This is a margin rule; it is not a proof that the physical constraint holds until the gate bounds are themselves supported.

