5.10 Gate P1.8 — Vehicle dynamics and damping

PMEDS vehicles may damp weakly, so a rigid-body model is not enough. Two-degree-of-freedom heave z and pitch θ, with levitation force F_L and pitch moment M_L:

m\ddot z=-c_z\dot z-k_z z+F_L(t), \qquad I_y\ddot\theta=-c_\theta\dot\theta-k_\theta\theta+M_L(t)

Target damping ratios ζ_z, ζ_θ ∈ [0.1, 0.7]. If passive damping falls short, active damping adds P_damp ∈ [0.2, 0.8] kW per vehicle, which enters P1.3 (upper envelope 0.8 kW). Stiffness and damping values are not yet specified. Acceptance: at least two degrees of freedom with stated damping ratios, and mass and power budgets that include damping hardware. Status [H].

Part III — Electrical Power Architecture

6. Thrust-Based Load Model

6.1 Mechanical dissipation is not grid power

The magnetic drag power P_D = F_D·v is mechanical dissipation in the track. The grid supplies the propulsion power needed to overcome magnetic and aerodynamic drag, divided by the motor efficiency, plus auxiliary, control and damping loads:

P_{prop}=\frac{(F_D+F_{aero})\,v}{\eta_{LIM}}, \qquad P_{veh}=P_{prop}+P_{aux}+P_{ctrl}+P_{damp}

6.2 Per-vehicle and corridor load

Under RC-1 and the Chapter 5 bands, the per-vehicle load is 3.26 kW (central) within [2.19, 6.36] kW. The corridor load is the sum over vehicles, P_corridor(t) = Σ P_veh,i(t); for stationary traffic of 20 vehicles it is 65.2 kW (central) within [43.8, 127.2] kW.

The model covers steady cruise only. Acceleration, standing vehicles and regeneration are outside it; regeneration is sized in Chapter 11.

6.3 Status

