7. Substation Architecture and Redundancy

7.1 Reference topology

Four substations in a line, each feeding one 250 m segment, with a DC interconnection between adjacent segments [H]. Each substation is an active-rectifier unit rated in kVA on the AC side.

7.2 Redundancy identities

Theorem 7.1 (redundancy overload, global sharing). Assume N identical substations carrying equal nominal shares of a total load P_tot, and that after the loss of k substations the load is shared equally by the N−k survivors. The relative overload of each survivor is:

\frac{P_i'-P_i}{P_i}=\frac{N}{N-k}-1=\frac{k}{N-k}

Proof. P_i = P_tot/N and P_i′ = P_tot/(N−k); divide the difference by P_i. ∎ Status [V] under the two assumptions.

Proposition 7.2 (local sharing in a line). Under the same equal segments, if the failed substation's segment is split equally between its adjacent substations, a single interior failure raises each of its two neighbours to 1.5 times its load, and a single end failure raises its one neighbour to 2.0 times. The worst-case relative overload is therefore 100%, three times the 33.3% of Theorem 7.1 for N = 4. Proof. An interior failure hands half of one segment to each neighbour (+50%). An end substation has one neighbour, which takes the whole segment (+100%). ∎ Status [V] under these assumptions.

Global sharing needs a DC bus able to move power along the whole kilometre; local sharing is the safer sizing basis and is used below.

Theorem 7.1 and Proposition 7.2 are identities about shares. They do not show that a physical network can carry the redistributed power. That needs four realisability conditions, which are separate [H] assumptions: R1 interconnection cables carry the redistributed current; R2 converters supply the overload (150% for 60 s); R3 voltage stays in [2700, 3300] V; R4 protection does not read the overload transient as a fault. The earlier "if and only if" wording mixed these with the theorem and is removed.

