9.3 Preliminary current-density rule

A_{th}\ge\frac{I_d}{J_{eff}\,f_{harm}}, \qquad J_{eff}=J_{air}\,f_{soil}\,f_{group}\,f_{dry}

With J_air = 3 A/mm², f_soil = 0.70, f_group = 0.85, f_dry = 0.80 and f_harm = 0.98 (all [H] rules of thumb, not IEC table values), J_eff = 1.428 A/mm² and the allowable density is 1.40 A/mm². The earlier text multiplied f_harm into J_eff and divided by it again, counting it twice. Corrected, A_th ≥ 31.8/1.40 = 22.7 mm², so the next standard size is 25 mm², with an allowable current of about 35 A and a margin of 1.10 over I_d. The combined rule is A = max(A_drop, A_th) = 25 mm² after rounding; the current-density rule governs.

9.4 What a real IEC 60287 calculation needs

This rule is not an IEC 60287 ampacity calculation. That calculation needs: conductor material and class; insulation material, thickness and thermal resistivity; sheath or armour; overall diameter; burial depth; soil thermal resistivity with dry-out behaviour; ambient temperature; spacing and grouping of the cable pair; loss terms including converter ripple; and the load cycle. Until those are fixed, the defensible claim is that a preliminary sizing method is specified and 25 mm² is a placeholder.

