11. Storage and Regenerative Braking

11.1 Kinetic energy and its three distinct quantities

For a vehicle of mass m at speed v:

E_{\mathrm{kin}}=\tfrac12 m v^2 = \tfrac12 \times 500 \times 8^2 = 16\ \mathrm{kJ}

Three quantities must be kept apart. They are not interchangeable.

1. Energy available for recovery. E_{\mathrm{avail}} = N_{\mathrm{brake}} \cdot E_{\mathrm{kin}}, where N_{\mathrm{brake}} is the number of vehicles braking in the corridor simultaneously. For N_{\mathrm{brake}} = 20: E_{\mathrm{avail}} = 320 kJ.
2. Energy actually recovered. E_{\mathrm{rec}} = \eta_{\mathrm{regen}} \cdot E_{\mathrm{avail}}, where \eta_{\mathrm{regen}} accounts for LIM generation efficiency, converter losses, and storage round-trip. With \eta_{\mathrm{regen}} = 0.70 [H]: E_{\mathrm{rec}} = 224 kJ. The earlier text mixed this 224 kJ with the 320 kJ total and produced an arithmetic contradiction; both figures are now labelled.
3. Peak regenerative power. P_{\mathrm{regen}}^{\mathrm{peak}} = E_{\mathrm{avail}} / T_{\mathrm{brake}} if all braking occurs over horizon T_{\mathrm{brake}}. For T_{\mathrm{brake}} = 2 s: P_{\mathrm{regen}} = 160 kW instantaneous. If braking is distributed over T_{\mathrm{brake}} = 2.76 s, the peak drops to 116 kW. RC-1 adopts P_{\mathrm{regen}}^{\mathrm{peak}} = 116 kW as the disturbance bound used in Gate P1.7; the 160 kW figure is retained as an unfiltered maximum.

The disturbance bound d_{\mathrm{regen}} \le 116 kW used in Gate P1.7 is thus the regenerative peak after averaging over the braking profile, not the instantaneous maximum.

11.2 Storage theorems

Theorem 11.1 (minimum supercapacitor energy). A supercapacitor of round-trip efficiency \eta_{\mathrm{SC}} that must absorb a power excursion \Delta P lasting T_{\mathrm{transient}} requires:

E_{\mathrm{SC}}\ \ge\ \frac{\Delta P \cdot T_{\mathrm{transient}}}{\eta_{\mathrm{SC}}}

Proof. The energy delivered by the SC to the bus is E_{\mathrm{out}} = \Delta P \cdot T_{\mathrm{transient}}. Stored energy must satisfy E_{\mathrm{SC}} \cdot \eta_{\mathrm{SC}} \ge E_{\mathrm{out}}. ∎ Status [V].

Theorem 11.2 (minimum supercapacitor power). The same SC must satisfy:

P_{\mathrm{SC}}\ \ge\ \Delta P

Proof. The SC must supply or absorb the instantaneous excursion. ∎ Status [V].

Theorem 11.3 (minimum battery energy). For a critical load requiring peak power P_{\mathrm{crit}}^{\mathrm{peak}} for duration t_{\mathrm{peak}} and continuous power P_{\mathrm{crit}}^{\mathrm{cont}} for the remainder of a ride-through of length T_{\mathrm{ride}}:

E_{\mathrm{bat}}\ \ge\ P_{\mathrm{crit}}^{\mathrm{peak}}\,t_{\mathrm{peak}} + P_{\mathrm{crit}}^{\mathrm{cont}}\,(T_{\mathrm{ride}} - t_{\mathrm{peak}})

Proof. The load profile is piecewise constant; total energy is the sum of the two intervals. ∎ Status [V].

11.3 Numerical sizing

Storage is placed at the substation (bus ride-through and regeneration absorption) and on board (vehicle ride-through and emergency propulsion). The following sizing uses RC-1 values.

Substation supercapacitor. For \Delta P = 116 kW, T_{\mathrm{transient}} = 0.5 s, \eta_{\mathrm{SC}} = 0.95:

E_{\mathrm{SC}} \ge \frac{116000 \times 0.5}{0.95} = 61.1\ \mathrm{kJ} = 0.017\ \mathrm{kWh}

A safety factor of 2 is adopted to cover a second coincident braking event without a second charging cycle:

E_{\mathrm{SC}}^{\mathrm{design}} = 0.034\ \mathrm{kWh}, \quad P_{\mathrm{SC}}^{\mathrm{design}} = 232\ \mathrm{kW}

The earlier 0.05 kWh / 320 kW is not derived from these numbers and is withdrawn.

Substation battery (ride-through). Ride-through target T_{\mathrm{ride}} = 30 s [H]. Continuous critical load: auxiliary and control of active vehicles during coasting, P_{\mathrm{crit}}^{\mathrm{cont}} = 6 kW (10 vehicles × 0.6 kW average, worst corridor scenario). No separate peak demand is added because the SC covers transients.

E_{\mathrm{bat}} \ge 6 \times 30 = 180\ \mathrm{kJ} = 0.05\ \mathrm{kWh}, \quad P_{\mathrm{bat}} \ge 6\ \mathrm{kW}

The earlier 0.5 kWh battery is ten times larger than this calculation supports.

On-board battery. Sized for one complete safe stop from cruise after loss of track supply, including auxiliary loads for the duration:

E_{\mathrm{on}} \ge \tfrac12 m v^2 + P_{\mathrm{aux}} \cdot t_{\mathrm{aux}} = 16\ \mathrm{kJ} + 1.0 \times 20 = 36\ \mathrm{kJ} = 0.010\ \mathrm{kWh}

A design margin of 4 gives 0.04 kWh, and the peak discharge is bounded by P_{\mathrm{aux}} + P_{\mathrm{ctrl}} = 1.2 kW. The earlier 1 kWh / 6 kW is not consistent with this duty cycle.

