12.3 Voltage ride-through theorem and the bus capacitance

Theorem 12.1 (bus capacitance for ride-through). For a step load increase \Delta P at constant generation, from V_0 to a floor V_{\min} over time T_{\mathrm{ride}}, the required bus capacitance is:

C_{\mathrm{bus}} \ge \frac{2\,\Delta P \cdot T_{\mathrm{ride}}}{V_0^2 - V_{\min}^2}

Proof. From C_{\mathrm{bus}} V \dot V = -\Delta P:

\int_{V_0}^{V_{\min}} V\,dV = -\frac{\Delta P}{C_{\mathrm{bus}}}\int_0^{T_{\mathrm{ride}}}dt,

\frac{V_{\min}^2 - V_0^2}{2} = -\frac{\Delta P\,T_{\mathrm{ride}}}{C_{\mathrm{bus}}},

C_{\mathrm{bus}} \ge \frac{2\,\Delta P\,T_{\mathrm{ride}}}{V_0^2 - V_{\min}^2}.

∎ Status [V] under the assumptions (constant generation during transient, negligible converter dynamics).

Numerical. With \Delta P = 100 kW (Gate P1.7 load step), T_{\mathrm{ride}} = 100 ms, V_0 = 3000 V, V_{\min} = 2700 V:

C_{\mathrm{bus}} \ge \frac{2 \times 100000 \times 0.1}{3000^2 - 2700^2} = 11.7\ \mathrm{mF}

The bus capacitance C_{\mathrm{bus}} = 12 mF used in RC-1 sits just above this bound. If the load step rises to 116 kW (regeneration) at the same ride-through time, the bound becomes 13.6 mF, above the RC-1 value. This is a design flag: C_{\mathrm{bus}} must be revised upward, or T_{\mathrm{ride}} reduced, if the regeneration step coincides with the ride-through. Listed in Appendix C.

12.4 Barrier certificate for the voltage constraint (example only)

This subsection shows the form a certificate would take, with placeholder coefficients. It does not assert a verified invariant.

Define \mathcal{K}_V = \{V \in [V_{\min}, V_{\max}]\} = [2700, 3300] V and B_V(V) = (V - V_c)^2 - (\Delta V)^2, with V_c = 3000 V and \Delta V = 300 V. Then \mathcal{K}_V = \{B_V \le 0\} and \nabla B_V = 2(V - V_c), which vanishes only at V = V_c, an interior point.

At V = V_{\min}, \nabla B_V \cdot f = 2(V_{\min} - V_c)\,(P_{\mathrm{gen}} - P_{\mathrm{load}})/(C_{\mathrm{bus}} V_{\min}). Since V_{\min} - V_c < 0, the barrier inequality \nabla B_V \cdot f \le 0 becomes P_{\mathrm{gen}} \ge P_{\mathrm{load}}. At V = V_{\max}, V_{\max} - V_c > 0 and the inequality becomes P_{\mathrm{gen}} \le P_{\mathrm{load}}.

The barrier certificate for \mathcal{K}_V therefore reduces to a control authority requirement: whenever V approaches a boundary, \mu must be able to bring P_{\mathrm{gen}} - P_{\mathrm{load}} to the correct sign within converter and SC power limits. This is a conditional certificate whose hypothesis (control authority available at the boundary) is a [H] to be verified against the converter and SC ratings of Chapters 7 and 11.

Extending the certificate to all four components of x requires four barrier functions and a check that the control authority requirements are mutually satisfiable. This is not done here.

