14. AGSK Kernel Specification

14.1 Formal interface

The AGSK is a deterministic function \Phi mapping a proposal and a measurement snapshot to an executable command:

u_t^{\mathrm{exec}} = \Phi\!\left(u_t^{\mathrm{AI}},\ x_t^{\mathrm{meas}},\ \pi_t\right)

where x_t^{\mathrm{meas}} is the measured state and \pi_t is the provenance record (see §14.7). The AGSK is defined by:

u_t^{\mathrm{exec}} = \begin{cases} u_t^{\mathrm{AI}} & \text{if } \mathrm{Adm}(u_t^{\mathrm{AI}}, x_t^{\mathrm{meas}}) = \mathrm{true} \\ u_k^{\mathrm{safe}} & \text{otherwise, with } k \text{ selected by the fallback table} \end{cases}

The admission predicate \mathrm{Adm} is a conjunction of physical constraints with margins from Chapter 4:

\mathrm{Adm}(u, x) = \bigwedge_{i=1}^{N_c} \Big[ g_i^{\mathrm{nom}}(x) + \varepsilon_i \le 0 \ \wedge \ g_i^{\mathrm{nom}}\big(f(x, u, d)\big) + \varepsilon_i \le 0 \ \ \forall d \in \mathcal{D} \Big]

where g_i^{\mathrm{nom}} is the nominal constraint function from the corresponding gate, and \varepsilon_i is the AGSK margin from Proposition 4.3 and the parameter uncertainties of Gates P1.1–P1.8.

Note. The quantification over d \in \mathcal{D} in \mathrm{Adm} is a design choice: the AGSK admits a command only if it is safe under all disturbances in the disturbance set. This is the conservative form. A less conservative form would use a measured disturbance distribution, but that would introduce a statistical assumption the thesis does not support.

14.2 The AGSK is not a language model

The AGSK is a deterministic finite-state automaton with an explicitly enumerated transition table. Language models remain at Layer 2 and Layer 3. No learned parameter enters the safety loop.

14.3 IEC 61508 compatibility

IEC 61508 advises against machine learning in systems targeting SIL 2 and above, because trained components lack the systematic capability evidence the standard requires. If the AGSK is to target SIL 3 or 4, it must be certified as a standalone safety instrumented system (SIS), with the Layer 2 and Layer 3 AI treated as a non-safety-related basic process control system.

Contract required before any SIL claim:

1. The SIL target for the AGSK safety function must be stated.
2. The AGSK must be an SIS independent of the AI layers, in hardware and software.
3. The interface from Layer 2 to the AGSK must be a black channel: no safety-critical data may flow from the AI into the AGSK other than a proposal, and the AGSK's decision must not depend on any internal state of the AI.
4. The AGSK must include diagnostics that detect its own failure modes and default to a safe state.
5. The AGSK's proof obligations must be discharged under a method acceptable to the standard for the target SIL.

This chapter states the contract. It does not discharge it.

14.4 Fallback table

Every fallback entry is a quadruple:

u_k^{\mathrm{safe}} = (\text{action},\ \mathcal{D}_k,\ \text{exit condition},\ \text{last-resort action})

· Action. The command issued when the AGSK rejects u^{\mathrm{AI}}.
· Domain \mathcal{D}_k. The set of states in which the fallback is valid. Outside \mathcal{D}_k, the fallback is not applied.
· Exit condition. The predicate that ends the fallback and returns control to nominal.
· Last-resort action. Applied when the exit condition cannot be met within the fallback domain: safe shutdown, sectionalisation, or controlled stop.

Requirement. For every failure mode in Gate P1.6, the quadruple must be complete. Acceptance is checked by enumeration; refutation is any failure mode lacking a bounded domain or a defined last resort.

14.5 Three proof levels and their scope

Level Domain Method What it establishes
Exhaustive FSM states Enumeration Universality over the finite-state abstraction
Abstract Continuous states Interval arithmetic, reachability Universality under abstraction, with a stated abstraction error
Formal Continuous states Barrier certificates, SOS, reachability with guaranteed enclosures Invariance with a mathematical certificate

Critical clarification. Exhaustive enumeration of an FSM does not, by itself, prove the safety of the continuous system. The link between the FSM abstraction and the continuous dynamics must be established separately, including measurement error, decision delay, and actuator latency. Without that link, the FSM proof is a proof about the FSM, not about the plant.

14.6 Constraint margins

For each physical constraint g_i(x) \le 0:

g_i^{\mathrm{AGSK}}(x) = g_i(x) + \varepsilon_i \le 0, \qquad \varepsilon_i = \varepsilon_i^{\mathrm{meas}} + \Lambda_i (T_s + \tau_i)

with \Lambda_i the Lipschitz rate bound of g_i along admissible trajectories, T_s the AGSK sampling period, and \tau_i the check-to-effect latency. Proposition 4.3 gives the derivation. The margin is not a proof that the physical constraint holds; it is a rule that makes the proof obligation explicit.

14.7 Command validation cycle

1. Authenticate the proposing agent, its permission level, and the provenance of its inputs.
2. Verify the electrical limits, temporal constraints, and equipment availability against the current measured state.
3. Evaluate the consequences of the proposed command on safety invariants, reserves, and other critical loads under the disturbance set.
4. Authorize, limit, or refuse according to the admission predicate and the fallback policy.
5. Confirm execution by independent measurement and log the decision.

14.8 Evidence record

Each decision produces a record:

E = (S, M, U, T, A, V, R, P, D, C)

S source agent; M measurements used; U uncertainty bounds; T timestamp; A proposed action; V model version; R referenced safety rules; P provenance; D kernel decision; C execution confirmation.

The evidence record is the unit of auditability. It does not replace the proof obligations of §14.5.

14.9 Conditional safety theorem

Theorem 14.1 (conditional). Let \mathcal{C} \subseteq \mathcal{K} be a robustly controlled invariant set for the plant dynamics f under the AGSK feedback \mu_{\mathrm{AGSK}}, with the disturbance set \mathcal{D}. Let the constraint margins satisfy Proposition 4.3, and let the AGSK's decision latency T_s and effect latency \tau be bounded as stated in §14.6. Then, for every initial state x_0 \in \mathcal{C}, every admissible disturbance d(\cdot) \in \mathcal{D}, and every AGSK decision sequence, the closed-loop trajectory satisfies g_i(x(t)) \le 0 for all t \ge 0 and all i.

Proof sketch. By Theorem 3.1(a), the invariance of \mathcal{C} under \mu_{\mathrm{AGSK}} ensures x(t) \in \mathcal{C} \subseteq \mathcal{K} for all t \ge 0. Since \mathcal{K} = \bigcap_i \{g_i \le 0\} and \varepsilon_i \ge 0, the physical constraint is satisfied. The margin rule accounts for the gap between measured and true constraint value at each sampling instant. ∎

Status of Theorem 14.1. The statement is [V] as a conditional theorem. Its hypotheses — existence of \mathcal{C}, of \mu_{\mathrm{AGSK}}, of the disturbance set \mathcal{D}, and of the Lipschitz bounds \Lambda_i — are not verified for RC-1. They are the content of Phase 2 of the falsification programme. The theorem is therefore a template, not a certificate.

14.10 Status

The AGSK specification is [H]: the interface, fallback structure, and evidence record are stated, but neither the admission predicate nor the invariant set is instantiated for RC-1. Theorem 14.1 is [V] only as a conditional implication.

