15. Threat Model

15.1 Adversary classes

Class Capability Countermeasure
Passive Listen, correlate traces Encryption, traffic analysis resistance
Active non-targeted Denial of service, jamming Redundancy, rate limiting, watchdog timers
Active targeted Compromised agent, false measurements Independent measurement chains, AGSK, cross-checking
Insider Legitimate operator bypassing procedure Two-person rule, independent logging, separation of duty
Model Plausible but false output from a learned model Deterministic AGSK, no learned component in the safety loop

The earlier text listed four classes; the model class is added here because the AGSK's safety claim depends on the AGSK being outside the learned component, and that boundary must be named.

15.2 Boundary conditions of the threat model

The following are out of scope for v2.0 and are listed in Appendix C:

· Physical tampering with the track, the vehicle, or the substation.
· Supply-chain attacks on the AGSK hardware or compiler.
· Coercion of an insider under duress.
· Coordinated multi-vector attacks across all five classes simultaneously.

15.3 Independence requirements

The independence requirements of §13.5 apply. A threat that compromises Agent E through the same channel that compromises Agent A is not detected, because the two are then correlated. Hardware, software, and measurement diversity are joint requirements.

15.4 Preemption hierarchy and its limits

The preemption hierarchy of §13.3 states precedence. It does not state that the AGSK can detect a compromised Layer 1 protection. If Layer 1 is compromised, the AGSK is downstream of the compromise. This is acknowledged as a boundary of the architecture: the AGSK assumes a correct Layer 1.

15.5 Status

Threat model [H]. It is a specification of the classes and their countermeasures, not an evaluation of their effectiveness against any real attacker.

