17. Conclusion

17.1 What this thesis establishes

The thesis specifies, at the level of mathematical structure and stated hypotheses, a cyber-physical architecture for PMEDS-enabled urban corridors in which a deterministic safety kernel validates commands proposed by multi-agent AI. The architectural claims are:

1. A corrected viability, invariance, and barrier-certificate framework (Part I) that distinguishes sufficiency from necessity and shows, by counterexample, why the earlier equality claim fails.
2. A nine-gate Physical Reference Model (Part II) with explicit parameter bands, validity domains, and epistemic statuses.
3. A single frozen electrical design case, RC-1, with reconciled load, substation allocation, bus voltage, cable, protection timing, and storage (Part III).
4. A three-layer control architecture with an AGSK specification, a conditional safety theorem, and an explicit threat model (Part IV).
5. A falsification programme with operational semantics for its comparative criterion (Chapter 16).

17.2 What this thesis does not establish

The thesis does not:

· Report any measurement, prototype, or simulation. No [S] result is claimed.
· Validate any physical parameter against a hardware test.
· Compute a robust invariance kernel, or any under-approximation of one.
· Discharge the AGSK's SIL obligations.
· Establish the economic feasibility of PMEDS for an urban road network.
· Resolve whether 3000 V DC is the correct bus voltage for a corridor carrying 16–32 A per feeder.

These are stated as open problems in Appendix C.

17.3 The central rule, restated with its limits

No AI shall be tasked with compensating for physics that has not yet been understood. No safety constraint is operative unless it is derivable from a physical result with explicitly bounded domain and uncertainty.

The architecture expresses this rule. It does not yet satisfy it, because the physical constraints the AGSK would enforce are still hypotheses. The value of the thesis is not that the architecture is safe; it is that the architecture makes explicit what would have to be true for it to be safe, and where the burden of proof lies at each step.

17.4 Next step

Phase 1 of Chapter 16: instantiate the nine gates in closed form with a reproducible script, and either promote each to [C] under stated hypotheses or leave it at [H] with an explicit parameter band. Only after Phase 1 closes can Phase 2 begin.

No physical experiment is required to advance. The falsification criteria are stated in terms of closed-form arithmetic and literature parameters. A script that reproduces the arithmetic in Appendix A is the deliverable.

